{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug Stage 1 (Gather) — interim finding, recorded before the cause is confirmed\n\nPosting now rather than at Phase 1 exit because it corrects a premise in this ticket's own\ndescription, and that correction should survive an interrupted session.\n\n### Correction: `REBAR_LLM_TIMEOUT` is NOT a wall-clock guard\n\nThe description says the knob is \"wired into both the Bedrock client\n(`src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:95` -> `read_timeout`/`connect_timeout`) and a wall-clock guard\n(`src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:376`)\". Reading `structured_run.py:376` on origin/main, that\nsecond site is not a wall-clock guard:\n\n```python\nif cfg.timeout_s:\n    model_settings[\"timeout\"] = float(cfg.timeout_s)\n```\n\n`model_settings[\"timeout\"]` is a pydantic-ai `ModelSettings` field mapping to the underlying\n**httpx request timeout** — the code comment at :370 says so explicitly (\"`timeout` is a base\nModelSettings field mapping to the underlying httpx/Anthropic client request timeout\"). The\nBedrock mapping is the same shape: botocore `read_timeout`/`connect_timeout`, i.e. per-socket-\nphase bounds.\n\n**So both wirings bound the MODEL HTTP CALL only. Nothing bounds the operation as a whole.**\nThe framing \"the timeout was bypassed\" is therefore wrong in a way that matters: the timeout was\nnever in scope for the region that hung. Every step after the model response — attestation\nsigning, snapshot materialization, sidecar/banking writes, store append, `git` commit, `git`\npush — runs with no time bound at all.\n\nThis is consistent with, and predicted by, observation 4: pid 33196 at **87.7% CPU** at t+1h18m\nand **0.0%** at t+3h03m. It finished compute inside the guarded region and then wedged in the\nunguarded one.\n\n### Supporting structural evidence: the unguarded region is large\n\nAn AST pass over `src/rebar` (every `subprocess.run` / `Popen` / `call` / `check_output` node,\nchecking for a `timeout` keyword) finds:\n\n- **93** subprocess call sites total\n- **61** with **no `timeout=`**\n- **54** of those also do not set `stdin`/`input`, so they inherit the parent's stdin and can\n  block on a prompt (credentials, an ssh passphrase, a host-key confirmation)\n\nConcentrated on exactly the post-LLM paths: every `git` call in `_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`\n(573, 591, 607), `llm/workflow/snapshot.py` (83, and a `Popen` at 181), `_store/push.py:209`,\n`llm/plan_review/sidecar.py:515`, `llm/workflow/completion_banking.py:380`,\n`llm/code_review/finalize.py:94`, `_commands/txn.py:154`, `_commands/close_precheck.py:328`,\n`attest/sshsig.py` (49, 80, 142), `_opcert_signing.py` (70, 122).\n\nA `subprocess.run` with no `timeout=`, blocked on a network read or an unanswerable prompt,\npresents as exactly the reported signature: **0.0% CPU, state `S`, indefinite**.\n\n### Status: hypothesis, NOT confirmed\n\nPer the protocol this is `dynamic` — a claim about what blocks at runtime — so it **cannot** be\nconfirmed by reading source, and I am not claiming it is. AC1 requires the exact blocking call be\nidentified by direct observation. No wedged process exists right now to dump.\n\nTwo things are in flight:\n1. A **py-spy watchdog** is installed at `~/rebar-hang-dumps/watch.sh` (running). It scans once a\n   minute for a `rebar review-plan|transition|verify-completion|review-code|sign-review` process\n   that has been alive >= 15 min at <= 1% CPU, and on a match writes `py-spy dump`, `py-spy dump\n   --locals`, and the process's open TCP/pipe/lock handles to `~/rebar-hang-dumps/hang-<pid>-<ts>.txt`.\n   It **captures and does not kill**, so the next occurrence is preserved rather than lost — this\n   is what will satisfy AC1.\n2. Parallel investigators are tracing the post-model call chain and attempting an experimental\n   reproduction of an unbounded block.\n\n### The open discriminator\n\nThe hypothesis must still explain observation 1: why did `review-plan` and `transition -> closed`\nhang while `comment`, `edit`, `link` and `set-file-impact` completed in seconds, when those also\nappend to the store and push to the remote? If the blocking call were simply `git push`, the plain\nwrites should have wedged too. Either the LLM-backed ops reach a call the plain writes never make\n(snapshot materialization and attestation signing are the obvious candidates — neither is on the\nplain-write path), or the hypothesis is wrong. Not yet resolved, and I am not treating it as\nresolved."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787251215862505001,"uuid":"0ee09459-fad2-4156-944e-2cfa0206f77e"}